# Caddy site block for X5 Commander. Replace game.example.com with your own domain.
#
# X5 Commander has a scoreboard API, so Caddy doesn't serve files itself: it terminates HTTPS (Let's Encrypt,
# automatic) and proxies everything to the Node server (server/index.ts), which serves the game, the API, gzip,
# caching and the security headers. The Node server runs as a systemd *user* service of the deploy user
# (deploy/x5-commander.service), so deploys restart it without sudo.
#
# One-time setup on the server (needs sudo, run interactively; DEPLOY_USER is the account you deploy as):
#
#   # Node 22.18+ (24 recommended) as /usr/bin/node, if it isn't there yet (node --version):
#   curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_24.x | sudo -E bash - && sudo apt-get install -y nodejs
#
#   sudo mkdir -p /var/www/x5-commander/releases /var/www/x5-commander/shared
#   sudo chown -R DEPLOY_USER:DEPLOY_USER /var/www/x5-commander
#   sudo loginctl enable-linger DEPLOY_USER    # user services keep running without a login session
#   sudo tee -a /etc/caddy/Caddyfile <<'EOF'
#
#   game.example.com {
#           reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8095
#   }
#   EOF
#   sudo systemctl reload caddy
#
# DNS: an A/AAAA (or CNAME) record for your domain pointing at the server.
#
# After that, deploys are just (or push to main: .github/workflows/deploy.yml):
#
#   X5_DEPLOY_HOST=DEPLOY_USER@your-server X5_DOMAIN=game.example.com ./deploy/deploy.sh
#
# and rollbacks:
#
#   X5_DEPLOY_HOST=DEPLOY_USER@your-server ./deploy/rollback.sh

game.example.com {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8095
}
